sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch02-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The intuition that complex objects require complex construction plans fails because it derives from engineering, where designers restrict themselves to systems whose behavior they can understand and predict, a constraint nature does not share.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Intuition that complex results need complex plans comes from engineering";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 2, “The Crucial Experiment”; section “The Need for a New Intuition” (from p. 39), p. 40. Scope: human intuition about complexity and the practice of engineering"
}
